    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let $x$ be the integer we are looking for. We want to find the only integer whose square is less than its double, so we can set up the following inequality: $x^2 < 2x$. \n\nRearranging the inequality, we get $x^2 - 2x < 0$. Factoring out an $x$, we have $x(x-2) < 0$. \n\nThe product of two numbers is negative if and only if one number is positive and the other is negative. So, we need to consider the cases where $x$ is positive and $x-2$ is negative, or vice versa.\n\nCase 1: $x > 0$ and $x-2 < 0$. In this case, we have $x > 0$ and $x < 2$. The only integer that satisfies both inequalities is $x = 1$.\n\nCase 2: $x < 0$ and $x-2 > 0$. In this case, we have $x < 0$ and $x > 2$. There are no integers that satisfy both inequalities.\n\nTherefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is $\\boxed{1}$.",
